INT. ICE-FISHING HUT - NIGHT

Plywood walls. A hole in the floor, black water, the surface skinned with ice. A propane lantern hisses. A tape recorder sits on a stool. The wind outside sounds like a throat.

The door shudders, bangs, opens. Snow swirls in.

GRETA (30s, parka caked white, ski mask pushed up, red-rimmed eyes) staggers inside, drags the door shut, and slides the bolt. She drops her pack. Rips off her gloves. The lantern flickers as if breathing in her fear.

She flips the tape recorder. The reel turns. A voice crackles.

GRETA (V.O.)
(recorded, bright)
Day three. Hut one. Everything is fine. I'm fine.

She slams the stop button. Presses her back against the wall. Listens. Wind rattles the tin chimney. Something scrapes across the roof, slow as a fingernail. Greta slides her knife from her boot and holds it level. The scraping moves down the wall, around the corner, to the door. The bolt trembles in its bracket. The lantern gutters low. She lifts the knife. The hole in the floor bubbles, a single fat bubble, and bursts.

GRETA
(whispering)
I'm fine.

The door handle turns. Slowly. Once. Twice. Then the whole hut lurches as if shouldered, and the lantern swings, throwing a long shadow across the ceiling. The knife trembles in her fist. She cuts the lantern's gas with her other hand, and darkness takes the room.

In the dark, the tape recorder clicks on by itself.

GRETA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded, calm)
Don't open the door.

GRETA
I didn't!

The bolt slides back on its own.
